## GATE (a) — the split, on the fixture the ratio is quoted on
elf 50ee709442b661b3c4495d8b4ccbadebe69a44f89b8732f9a31c672353f8d13d, loadavg 34.54/35.57/36.16
  factor_p50 = 13.003 ms   sixteen_solves_p50 = 6.887 ms   solve_share = 0.3462
Repeated across four later builds/windows the share sits in 0.331-0.490; the three
runs with a settled host give 0.3313, 0.3346, 0.3586. So the solve is ~33-35% of
this cell, NOT the ~31% banked at n=1,728. My own prediction was that the share
would FALL with n. It did not. Recorded that way round because the prediction was
wrong.

## LOSS (reverted): `y` built by `push` instead of `vec![0.0; n]`
  push-built   sixteen_solves_p50 = 10.053992 ms   (factor 10.459251 ms)
  zeroed       sixteen_solves_p50 =  5.769022 ms   (factor 10.316419 ms)
The factorization is untouched by either arm and moved 1.4% between the windows,
so it is the control: the host was flat and the substitution was 42.6% slower.
Cause: `y[col]` against a Vec whose `len` grows each iteration is a bounds check
against a MOVING bound, which LLVM cannot hoist; a fixed-length buffer's can be.
One n-element memset traded for a per-nonzero check. REVERTED in 90c30c285.

## IN-FLOOR (no claim): composing the rhs index maps
`b[fill_perm[row_perm[row]]]` instead of materializing the permuted rhs. Removes
one n-element allocation and one n-element write per solve, bit-identical.

Paired ABBA, both arms in ONE binary via SPLU_SOLVE_FORCE_MATERIALIZED_RHS,
dispatch_observed=true, 21 replicates:

  elf 2a4e8b0d5e6d5487dc172174aa215ee8a26612793688fc96fc19468d80030b0b (fixed schedule)
    AB(materialized/composed) median 1.125842 p10-p90 [1.016295,1.276655]
    solve A/A                 median 1.185360 p10-p90 [1.008266,1.514333]   IN-FLOOR

  elf 255506556d6b1a74964be2211810f5b43e94a600955f059a9c654198381213da (position-balanced)
    AB(materialized/composed) median 0.983687 p10-p90 [0.724727,1.381388]
    solve A/A                 median 1.584377 p10-p90 [1.112313,1.894612]   IN-FLOOR

The first schedule always put the ORIG arm in the cold outer slot and reported the
candidate at 1.126 -- against a null of 1.185. That was the SCHEDULE, not the code.
Balancing the quartet (ABBA on even rounds, BAAB on odd) moved the candidate to
0.98 and left the null at 1.58.

VERDICT: no effect measurable. The lever is smaller than the probe's floor and the
floor is not noise -- it is a real, repeatable cold/warm effect worth ~1.6x on a
sixteen-solve pass depending on whether the factor was just built.

## WHAT THAT IMPLIES FOR THE NEXT LEVER, and it is the useful part
At n=4,096 the dominant variable in this solve is MEMORY STATE, not arithmetic or
allocation: an identical sixteen-solve pass runs ~1.6x faster when the factor's
rows are already resident. Removing an n-element write cannot move a job whose
spread is dominated by whether 5.75 MB of factor is in cache.

So the remaining solve-side item on this bead -- converting `l_rows`/`u_rows` from
jagged `Vec<Vec<(usize, f64)>>` to flat u32/f64 parallel arrays -- should be
attacked as a BYTES-TOUCHED change, not as an indexing change: it halves the index
stream (u32 against a 16-byte pair with a usize) and makes the rows contiguous,
which is the variable this probe says actually moves. Size it against the ~33-35%
the solve is worth, and expect the same 1.6x floor to require a paired,
position-balanced probe to see through.

NOT ESTABLISHED: any whole-job ratio for either change. The vs-incumbent cell was
re-measured separately (tests/artifacts/perf/run7d-convection-side64-remeasure.log)
and nothing in this file claims to move it.
